ge of the image.)Tj-18 -16 TD (\245)Tj/F5 10 Tf18 0 TD (Outlier noise. )Tj/F6 10 Tf69.387 0 TD (This category of noise includes salt and pepper noise \(see )Tj-69.387 -12 TD (above\). The distribution histogram of this type of noise displays noise )Tj0 -12 TD (peaks at the high and low ends of the range.)Tj-18 -20 TD (After you have identi\336ed the type of noise, go to Step 2.)Tj/F7 11 Tf-9 -27.5 TD (Step 2: Select Filter Size)Tj/F6 10 Tf9 -20.5 TD (Image noise is \336ltered by means of a \336ltering window \(or kernel\), which is )Tj0 -12 TD -0.109 Tw (measured in pixels. This \336ltering window slides across the image, processing )TjT* 0 Tw (the pixels within it.)Tj0 -20 TD -0.321 Tw (The available \336lter dimensions range from 3)Tj/F1 10 Tf193.29 0 TD 0 Tw ( x )Tj/F6 10 Tf9.93 0 TD (3 pixels to 9)Tj/F1 10 Tf51.381 0 TD ( x )Tj/F6 10 Tf9.93 0 TD -0.515 Tw (9 pixels. To select )Tj-264.532 -12 TD 0 Tw (an appropriate size, magnify a background region of your image so that you )Tj0 -12 TD (can see the individual pixels. The \336lter size you select should be larger than )TjT* (the average noise feature but smaller than your data features.)TjETendstreamendobj1098 0 obj3190endobj1096 0 obj<</Type /Page/Parent 1084 0 R/Resources <</Font <</F1 7 0 R /F4 16 0 R /F5 18 0 R /F6 20 0 R /F7 25 0 R >>/ProcSet 2 0 R>>/Contents 1097 0 R>>endobj1100 0 obj<</Length 1101 0 R>>stream
BT/F4 12 Tf0 Tc 0 Tw 108.125 662.5 TD (Quantity One User Guide)Tj/F4 10 Tf167.217 -530.75 TD [ (10-)1 (32)] TJ/F11 9 Tf-167.217 500.5 TD (Note:)Tj/F6 10 Tf36 0 TD -0.023 Tc (A smaller \336lter will alter your image less than a larger \336lter. Large \336lters )Tj0 -12 TD 0 Tc (can result in better suppression of noise, but can also blur desirable )TjT* (features in the image.)Tj/F7 11 Tf-27 -27.5 TD (Step 3: Begin Filtering)Tj/F6 10 Tf9 -20.5 TD (After you have completed your selections, the \336lter name and size will be )Tj0 -12 TD (displayed at the bottom of the Filter Wizard dialog box.)Tj0 -20 TD -0.015 Tc (To being \336ltering, click on the OK button. Because \336ltering is an irreversible )Tj0 -12 TD 0 Tc (process, a pop-up box will give you the option of \336ltering the original image, )TjT* (creating a copy of the image to \336lter, or cancelling out of this operation.)Tj0 -20 TD (If you choose to Copy and Filter, you will be asked to enter a new name and/)Tj0 -12 TD (or version number for the new copy before the operation is performed. Once )TjT* (this information has been entered, the \336ltering operation will be performed.)Tj/F7 12 Tf-18 -24.25 TD (10.9.b. Selecting a Filter Directly)Tj/F6 10 Tf18 -20.75 TD (If you already know the type and size of \336lter you want, you can select it )Tj0 -12 TD (directly by selecting Image > Filters > Filter List. The pulldown menu )TjT* (includes all the \336lters available in the Discovery Series.)Tj0 -20 TD (The types of \336lters in the Discovery Series are:)Tj0 -18 TD (\245)Tj/F5 10 Tf18 0 TD (Weighted Mean.)Tj/F6 10 Tf78.865 0 TD ( This \336lter is useful for reducing Gaussian noise. It )Tj-78.865 -12 TD (calculates the weighted mean of the pixels within the \336ltering window )Tj0 -12 TD (and uses it to replace the value of the pixel being processed.)Tj-18 -16 TD (\245)Tj/F5 10 Tf18 0 TD (Out of Range Pixel.)Tj/F6 10 Tf93.864 0 TD -0.183 Tw ( This \336lter is useful for suppressing salt-and-pepper )Tj-93.864 -12 TD 0 Tw (noise; its effect on Gaussian noise is minimal. This \336lter calculates the )Tj0 -12 TD -0.466 Tw (mean of the pixel values in the \336ltering window, including the pixel being )TjT* 0 Tw (processed. If the difference between the mean and the individual pixel )TjT* (value is above a certain threshold, then the individual value is replaced )TjT* (by the mean.)Tj-18 -16 TD (\245)Tj/F5 10 Tf18 0 TD (Median.)Tj/F6 10 Tf40.078 0 TD ( Also useful for suppressing salt-and-pepper noise, this \336lter )Tj-40.078 -12 TD -0.123 Tw (calculates the median value of the pixels within the \336ltering window and )Tj0 -12 TD -0.147 Tw (uses it to replace the value of the pixel being processed. The median \336lter )TjT* 0 Tw (produces very little blurring if a small-sized window is selected.)TjETendstreamendobj1101 0 obj2855endobj1099 0 obj<</Type /Page/Parent 1084 0 R/Resources <</Font <</F4 16 0 R /F5 18 0 R /F6 20 0 R /F7 25 0 R /F11 76 0 R >>/ProcSet 2 0 R>>/Contents 1100 0 R>>endobj1103 0 obj<</Length 1104 0 R>>stream
